Release every command buffer and pass encoder, and destroy device buffers when they are freed.

halide_webgpu_run finishes a command encoder into a command buffer, submits it and releases
only the encoder; the command buffer and the compute pass encoder are never released. The
staging copy in do_copy_to_host releases neither its encoder nor its command buffer, and the
device-to-device copy keeps its command buffer. A submitted command buffer that is never
released keeps every buffer it referenced alive, and under Emscripten each leaked handle is
also an entry in the JS-side object table. Measured in Chrome on an Apple GPU, a frame of 64
dispatches slowed from ~80 ms to ~450 ms over forty frames in one page as the leaked
intermediates accumulated; with the releases in place the frame time holds.

halide_webgpu_device_free also destroys the buffer before releasing it. Destruction is
deferred past any work already submitted against the buffer, so it is safe, and it gives the
memory back now rather than whenever the last reference elsewhere lets go.

Sent nowhere yet; it belongs upstream on Halide PR #8955, which this tree is a checkout of,
and applies on top of halide-webgpu-pipeline-cache.patch.
